<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yarkand</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yarkant, Shache</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yarkand, also called Shache, is an oasis city in the south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, on the Yarkand River. A station on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> routes to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bactria">Bactria</a>, it was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a> from 1514 until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungar</a> conquest of 1680, and later a major town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgaria">Kashgaria</a> under Qing rule. Its people are mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghurs</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yarkand.jpg" alt="Yarkand" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colegota · CC BY-SA 2.5 es · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yarkand-calles-d01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Yarkand, or Yarkant County, lies on the southern rim of the Taklamakan Desert in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a>, at an altitude of 1,189 metres, and is administered under Kashgar Prefecture. The oasis is fed by the Yarkand River, which flows north from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karakorum">Karakorum</a> and through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kunlun-mountains">Kunlun Mountains</a>. It now covers 3,210 square kilometres but was probably far more extensive before a period of desiccation that began from the 3rd century CE. The oasis farmland produces <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, wheat, corn, pomegranates, pears, apricots and walnuts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-han">Book of Han</a> of the 1st century BCE first mentions the territory as Shaju, probably related to the name of the Iranian Saka tribes. In the 1st century CE the kingdom of Suoju was the most powerful in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-regions">Western Regions</a>. Its king Xian, given the seal of Protector General in 41 CE and then denied it, conquered Kucha and other kingdoms, until Guangde of Khotan captured him in 61 CE and killed him. The Han general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-chao">Ban Chao</a> defeated Suoju in 86 CE, and in 130 CE Yarkand sent tribute to the Chinese emperor along with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>.</p><p>Little is recorded for many centuries after that. Yarkant became the main base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khan">Chagatai Khan</a>, who inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgaria">Kashgaria</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a> died in 1227. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> described it in 1273 as a province where people followed the law of Mahomet, with some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-the-east">Nestorian Christians</a>, and rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>. In 1509 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dughlat">Dughlats</a> rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moghulistan">Moghulistan</a>, and five years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-said-khan">Sultan Said Khan</a> conquered them and set up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a>, with Yarkand as its capital. The Jesuit Benedict Göez reached the city in 1603 and called it a mart of much note, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> caravan ended and a new one formed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cathay">Cathay</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a> gained control of the region in the middle of the 18th century. By the 19th century trade with Ladakh and foreign merchants made it the largest and most populous state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashgar">Kashgar</a>. In the 1860s Yakub Beg made Yarkant the capital of his Turkic state of Yettishar and received embassies from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1870 and 1873. The Qing defeated him in 1877, and he committed suicide. Yarkant is now predominantly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a>, with a small Tajik minority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yarkant County” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1680 the cities of Altishahr, among them Kashgar, <b>Yarkand</b> and Khotan, paid the Dzungars substantial tribute in grain, cloth and silver.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Hunza's Burushaski-speaking people farmed irrigated terraces and, until the late 19th century, raided caravans on the routes between Ladakh and <b>Yarkand</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/hunza-state">Hunza State</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1514 Sultan Said Khan, a descendant of Yunus Khan of Moghulistan, took Kashgar and <b>Yarkand</b> from the Dughlat amir Mirza Aba Bakr, founding a khanate with Yarkand as its capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/yarkent-khanate">Yarkent Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>Yaqub Beg soon sidelined Buzurg Khan, conquered Kashgar, <b>Yarkand</b> and Khotan, and by 1870 controlled most of the Tarim Basin, including Turpan and Urumqi.</blockquote><a href="/state/yettishar">Yettishar</a></li></ul></section>
